# Example 5: shipping the outputs as ppk packages
#
# This takes example 2's outputs and ships them as two ppk packages:
#
# - dev.example.greet, the program, installed as /usr/bin/greet;
# - dev.example.greet-dev, for developers who want to use the library:
#   /usr/include/greet.h and /usr/lib/libgreet.a.
#
# One build makes two packages. These are split packages, as in the kernel
# recipe in pkgs/. This example contains a copy of example 2's sources and
# build.gcsl. The packages are in this file, package.gcsl, beside it.
#
# WHAT MUST HAPPEN
#
# By hand, after building example 2:
#
#   mkdir -p greet/bin greet-dev/include greet-dev/lib       # 1. stage each package's
#   cp app greet/bin/greet                                   #    files, laid out as
#   cp lib/greet.h greet-dev/include/                        #    they will be under
#   cp libgreet.a greet-dev/lib/                             #    /usr
#
#   ppk pack 'dev.example.greet@1.0-1?arch=x86_64' greet \
#       --artifact-dir artifacts                             # 2. pack each one
#   ppk pack 'dev.example.greet-dev@1.0-1?arch=x86_64' greet-dev \
#       --artifact-dir artifacts
#
# This was run by hand. Packing is deterministic: the same files packed into a
# fresh artifact directory give the same identity (sha256:7b27eb46...). So a
# pack can be cached like any other step.
#
#   You change                  Must rerun                  Must NOT rerun
#   main.c                      app's compile and link,     the greet-dev pack,
#                               then the greet pack         because its files
#                                                           didn't change
#   lib/greet.c                 everything, then both packs
#   only the package version    both packs                  every compile and link
#   a comment in main.c,        app's compile               everything after it,
#     giving an identical app                               through early cutoff
#
# WHAT'S NEW
#
# 1. Packing is a step; publishing isn't. ppk pack writes into an artifact
#    directory, a store shared with everything else that writes there. A
#    hermetic step can't write into a store outside its sandbox. So:
#
#    - The pack step packs into a fresh, empty artifact directory in its own
#      sandbox and returns it as its output. That output is cached and
#      deterministic.
#    - Copying it into a real store, such as yours, the system's or
#      pkgs.peios.org, is a separate action that changes the world outside the
#      build.
#
#    The copy itself is easy, because blobs and manifests are
#    content-addressed. The exception is refs: ppk refuses a version whose
#    content has changed.
#
#    The open question is what command does that copy, and how it's written.
#    Bazel separates "bazel build", which never touches the outside world, from
#    "bazel run", which does something with the result. Here it might be a
#    different def-type, such as "def publish", run with something like
#    "pekit publish".
#
# 2. Staging is a map from /usr paths to files. That's the same shape as
#    fs.compose_dir from example 4, so it may be the same function.
#
# 3. Versions. ppk never lets a version change its content, but pekit rebuilds
#    whenever an input changes. If you edit main.c and rebuild without changing
#    the version, the pack step produces a different package with the same
#    version. That's fine in pekit-out/, but publishing it would be refused.
#    Today's pekit handles this by distinguishing release from development
#    builds and marking the latter +dirty. Where should the version of a
#    development build come from?
#
# 4. ppk is a library, not part of pekit. Something like gcslib/ppk wraps
#    ppk pack in a hermetic function. The ppk tool is an input FileSet, like the
#    compiler. This example tests that nothing here needs pekit to know what
#    ppk is.
#
# 5. Using the build's targets. The canonical layout (Jack) is a build.gcsl and
#    a package.gcsl in the same directory. Files in one directory see each
#    other's definitions, so this file can write build main() with no import.
#
# 6. On pekit's side: the executable bit. ppk records which files are
#    executable. You can see it in the artifact directory, where blob names end
#    in .exec. So a File's fingerprint must include whether it's executable,
#    not just its bytes. Bazel's remote cache protocol does the same. No syntax
#    is needed.
#
# The architecture is fixed as x86_64 for now. Example 6 is about that.
#
# WHAT TO WRITE
#
# Things to decide:
#
# - How a package is written: its name, version and files.
# - The pack step, if you want to write the library side.
# - How publishing is written and triggered, or leave it out and just say how
#   you'd want it.

# CLAUDE'S DRAFT, after Jack's proposal: a package is format-neutral metadata,
# and a packager library, selected in workspace configuration, turns it into a
# format and publishes it. Nothing below mentions ppk except the library and
# the configuration.
#
# Canonical layout (Jack): a directory holds build.gcsl and package.gcsl, and
# files in one directory see each other's definitions, so build main() needs
# no import.

let version = "1.0-1"

def package dev.example.greet() {
    version = version
    files = {
        "bin/greet" = build main()
    }
}

def package dev.example.greet-dev() {
    let g = build greet()
    version = version
    files = {
        "include" = g.headers.rebase("lib") # lib/greet.h becomes include/greet.h
        "lib/libgreet.a" = g.archive
    }
}

## in workspace.gcsl: which packagers this workspace uses. The command line
## can override it, and several can be listed to build every format at once.

pekitconf: PekitConfig = {
    packagers = [ppk.packager(tool = build ppk_tool())]
}

## in github.com/gcslib/ppk

import fs

# What pekit calls for each package when ppk is a selected packager. It is
# hermetic, so the package is cached like any step, and it packs into a fresh
# artifact directory inside its own sandbox.
#[hermetic]
def pack(name: String, pkg: Package, tool: FileSet, arch: String) -> FileSet {
    let sys = fs.compose_dir({
        "/" = tool
        "/work/in" = fs.compose_dir(pkg.files)
    }).sandbox(cwd = "/work")
    sys.exec("ppk", "pack", "{name}@{pkg.version}?arch={arch}", "in", "--artifact-dir", "out")
    = sys.dir("/work/out")
}

# What `pekit publish` calls. It is not hermetic: it copies a packed package
# into a real artifact store, outside the build. ppk's own rule applies there:
# a version whose content has changed is refused.
def publish(packed: FileSet, store: String) -> {} {
    # ...
}

